Z||000011||Gold-86SE||05-08-2000||10:07 AM||||<BR>Monkeyman, I agree totally.<P>The bottom line is that the Fiero out sold the 'vette every year the Fiero was in production. Also, the Fiero turned a profit every year it was produced, including 1988.<P>Don't let anyone tell you that slagging sales killed it.  This is just not true.<P>If the idea behind the Fiero was so terrible and costly, why in the world would GM use that development structure to produce and keep producing the Saturn.<P>The 'vette guys are god-like and believe that no other GM product should beat, better yet, not even think about beating the 'vette.  The 'vette is how GM wants to define sports car, and no competion is allowed within the GM ranks.<P>Here is another way to look at this little GM quandry.  Liken the 'vette to Apple Computers, and the Fiero to the MicroSoft Computers.<P>Apple makes a few computers and sells them at a high/higher cost than MS.  MS makes computers in high quantity and sells them at a low price.  In essence, you have two differnect and distinct marking styles.  Each makes money, but affects the market differently.<P>GM wants 'vette to be the egivalent of Apple Computers in the sports car automarket.  Chevy is to sell a few 'vettes at a high market price to turn a profit.  On the other hand, Pontiac was selling an inexpensive commuter/sports car that many people could afford, while still turning a company profit.<P>We all know what the MS marketing style did to the Apple Computers, MS almost put Steve Jobs and the boys out-o-business.  Sure the die-in-the-wool Apple people think the Apple Computer is better, but the MS people think otherwise, the same holds true for the 'vette and Fiero owners respectively.<P>Had GM allowed the Fiero to continue production it is quite possible that the Fiero would prove to be quite an obstacle in the sports car market, period.<P>You guys with Apple Computers, do not take it as a flame, and please do not change the subject - this was only an example.<BR>||||reg||3
