Z||000013||Oreif||05-08-2000||11:01 AM||||The Demise of the Fiero had nothing to do with safety. GM corporate never liked the Fiero. First the factory was owned by the employees not GM. The Fiero cutting into the Corvette sales is also not true. The mid 80's were the worst years for vettes in terms of power. Second, GM corporate wanted to get into the mini-van explosion that Chrysler started and they needed the machines that made Fiero's to make the panels for the vans. Finally, The agreement with the Fiero project was they had to sell 30,000 units per year to remain open. GM corporate cut advertising money by more than 70%. Ever notice the lack of commercials and magazine advertisements for 88's? If they advertised all the changes to the 88's (suspension, brakes, etc) most people would have bought a new one! Also back then the average new car buyer always bought a new car every 4.5 years. If there would have been an 89 Fiero sales would have been high again! Even with the cut in advertising dollars they still sold over 26,000 cars! <BR>The Saturn connection is that the same management that ran the Fiero project was offered the Saturn project upon closing the Fiero plant. All the mistakes that were made contract wise were changed to allow Saturn full control. They also took one of the machines for making the plastic panels. (GM kept 2 to make the vans) Just a few years ago the GM vans were re-designed with metal bodies and Saturn acquired the other 2 machines. One is in use to make the new LS series. Maybe Saturn will decide to make a mid-engine sports car again with the 3rd machine! (rumor is they want a small SUV)||||reg||1
Z||000014||Pontiaddict||05-18-2000||06:49 PM||||The Corvette people are stupid. They are holding back the HP on the LS1's because they  don't want to make the ZR1's LT5 look bad.<BR>Last I checked they didn't make ZR1's anymore. So why should they care if the lighter cheaper LS1's make more power? The ZR1 owners won't care because their cars are still worth a fortune. If they bumped the LS1  up to 400 hp, they would sell more cars, but their backwards thinking keeps them from making GM better. A new F-body will keep up with and, with a mod or two, beat a new vette. Gee, I wonder why the f-cars are going  extinct. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||fierosound||05-18-2000||07:34 PM||||Anyone remember the Buick Grand National.  It cost substantially less than the 'Vette and was blowing them away at the race track from the showroom floor.  It also had a turbo'd V6 engine which further pissed off the 'Vette owners who were of the "V8 crowd" and which mentality still exists today.  They loudly complained to GM about their overpriced, low performance 'Vettes.  GM didn't want a repeat with the Fiero/'Vette comparison.  Another reason the V6 turbo unit slated for '89/'90 never made it.||||reg||3
